                <h2>Part 1: Ad Creative Compliance Check (48 Hours Before Launch)</h2>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Core Principle</strong>: Creative compliance is the first line of defense. Most account bans start with creative violations.</p>
</blockquote>
<ul>
<li>[ ] <strong>IP Rights Verification</strong>: Do you have copyright for all images/videos? Is theBGM commercially licensed? Are competitor logos or IP characters appearing in your creatives?</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Exaggeration Filter</strong>: Have you removed absolute claims ("best," "#1," "100%")? Do performance promises ("guaranteed weight loss," "results in 3 days") have data backing?</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Sensitive Element Scan</strong>: Have you avoided content involving religion, race, or political leanings? Do you have model releases for any people depicted?</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Platform-Specific Rules</strong>: TikTok strictly restricts call-to-action phrases that induce engagement (likes, shares); Meta prohibits before-and-after images for weight loss/fitness ads.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Action Tip</strong>: Build a creative compliance scoring sheet and have two people cross-check each asset before archiving.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Part 2: Landing Page and Claim Consistency Audit</h2>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Does the landing page URL match the ad? Does the destination page actually exist?</li>
<li>[ ] Do promotional offers (discounts, gifts, limited-time deals) in the ad exactly match what's on the landing page?</li>
<li>[ ] Is the privacy policy page accessible and clickable from the landing page footer? Does it comply with regional regulations (GDPR, CCPA, etc.)?</li>
<li>[ ] Do your purchase/registration flows include clear cancellation and refund policies?</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Common Pitfall</strong>: The advertiser promotes "free trial" but buries the paid terms on a second-level page. Such inconsistencies trigger user complaints and tank account health scores.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Part 3: Regional and Legal Compliance Mapping</h2>
<blockquote>
<p>Advertising laws vary significantly by region. Build compliance matrices by market tier.</p>
</blockquote>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Check Item</th>
<th>Europe/US</th>
<th>Southeast Asia</th>
<th>Middle East</th>
<th>Japan/Korea</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Privacy Data Collection</td>
<td>Explicit consent popups required</td>
<td>Relatively lenient but requires registration</td>
<td>Must comply with local Islamic law</td>
<td>Must disclose data usage purpose</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Child-Targeting</td>
<td>Prohibited under 13</td>
<td>Some countries restrict under 16</td>
<td>Strictly limited</td>
<td>Parental consent required</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Finance/Gambling</td>
<td>Strict licensing required</td>
<td>Some countries open</td>
<td>Mostly prohibited</td>
<td>License required</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Practical Move</strong>: Before entering a new market, contact your account manager for recent policy change alerts in that region.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Part 4: Account Structure Compliance Review</h2>
<ul>
<li>[ ] For multi-account operations, are you using independent IPs, device fingerprints, and payment methods? Has shared information been detected by platforms as related accounts?</li>
<li>[ ] Does your Business Manager follow the principle of least privilege in permission assignment? Have you removed access for departed employees promptly?</li>
<li>[ ] Do your account name, avatar, and description contain others' trademarks or prohibited terms?</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Warning Sign</strong>: When one ad account gets banned for violations, other accounts under the same Business Manager often get caught in the crossfire. Plan your account isolation architecture in advance.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Part 5: Routine Audit Schedule and Response Protocols</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Frequency</th>
<th>What to Check</th>
<th>Response Timeframe</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Daily</td>
<td>Account health notifications, creative review status</td>
<td>Handle rejected creatives within 2 hours</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Weekly</td>
<td>Unusual data fluctuations, brand safety reports</td>
<td>Investigate potential new rule triggers</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Monthly</td>
<td>Policy update summaries, competitor compliance cases</td>
<td>Update internal compliance knowledge base</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<hr />
<h2>Part 6: Rapid Response When Issues Occur</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Screenshot Evidence</strong>: Immediately capture complete screenshots of ad rejections or account anomalies</li>
<li><strong>Parallel Appeals</strong>: Submit official appeals while contacting your account service provider for escalation support</li>
<li><strong>Root Cause Analysis</strong>: Distinguish between policy misjudgment and actual violations; adjust and resubmit</li>
<li><strong>Post-Incident Review</strong>: Add this case to your compliance checklist to prevent recurrence</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>Closing</h2>
<p>Compliance isn't a one-time task—it's a continuous audit throughout your entire campaign lifecycle. We recommend solidifying the checklist above into a formal SOP, supplemented by creative management tools and account monitoring dashboards, to shift from reactive firefighting to proactive risk prevention.</p>
